#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfdb7c3-fb14-545a-84b4-cc17cf2352b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292713} {:answer/id #uuid "2009f87b-8f15-570f-9bda-1b41c9aee810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292714} {:answer/id #uuid "aabfc78e-66e6-5edf-820f-dd3ddd4bbfe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292715} {:answer/id #uuid "3de33f0b-6fbd-5284-a6a7-464996ab991e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292716} {:answer/id #uuid "ef101227-9347-5b0f-887a-58c6b7c4a2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabfc78e-66e6-5edf-820f-dd3ddd4bbfe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292715} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ea3d48-f723-5f62-8215-c2767ccc9abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68cf6bb9-4815-5cbd-af07-6f1b5adb668a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122287" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}+(3)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)] \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
